WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.670
After Star Wars: The Force Awakens, moviegoers
around the world fell in love with Poe Dameron:

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.580
a loyal soldier, a good friend, and a heroic
leader.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.200
He's not afraid to crack jokes when squaring
off against lightsaber-wielding psychos, and

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.400
the dude can fly any ship you put in front
of him, whether it's an X-wing or a TIE fighter.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.060
But best of all, Dameron is played by Oscar
Isaac, one of the most talented actors in

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:22.920
Hollywood today.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.780
And while Isaac might be best known for Star
Wars, his resume includes everything from

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.120
indie thrillers to superhero blockbusters.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.020
Here are all the places you've seen Poe Dameron
before.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.260
The Nativity Story

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.660
For his fifth feature film, Oscar Isaac landed
the part of Joseph, one of the most important

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.300
but least-acknowledged characters to have
been involved in the birth of Jesus.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.040
Despite that lack of background info, Isaac
totally succeeds in bringing this Biblical

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:54.660
character to life, playing Joseph as a virtuous
man who sticks by his betrothed, Mary, even

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.640
when she mysteriously becomes pregnant with
the son of God.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.380
Body of Lies

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.780
One of Ridley Scott's lesser-known films,
Body of Lies follows a CIA agent played by

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.280
Leonardo DiCaprio who's hunting insurgents
across the Middle East.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.060
But despite his fluent Arabic and scraggly
beard, DiCaprio can't win the entire Iraq

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.140
War by himself, so he needs a bit of backup
from his sidekick and field operative, Bassam.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.640
Sporting a ponytail and a machine gun, Bassam
is the guy who covers DiCaprio when the going

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.280
gets rough.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.960
Unfortunately, he also doesn't live very long,
getting blown into little bits by an RPG during

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.620
the first act.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.420
But despite his limited screen time, Isaac
totally holds his own against DiCaprio...

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.540
right up until he's turned into human toast.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.380
Robin Hood

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.740
Just a few years after Body of Lies, Oscar
Isaac re-teamed with Ridley Scott for a grittier

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.420
take on England's most famous outlaw, played
by Russell Crowe.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.220
This film didn't do especially well with critics
or audiences, but Isaac's sleazy turn as the

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.480
infamous Prince John was a standout.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.920
As a man obsessed with taxing peasants and
cheating on his wife, Isaac took full advantage

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.680
of every opportunity to chew the scenery,
stand around naked, and generally come off

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:09.680
as a royal jerk.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.900
"You have a king and the runt of the litter
to call you mother."

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:16.760
Sucker Punch

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.180
This 2011 film by Zack Snyder tells the story
of Babydoll, played by Emily Browning, who's

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.760
framed for murder and shipped off to a horrific
1950s mental asylum.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.560
In order to cope, she escapes into her subconscious
— where she encounters dragons, Nazi robots,

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.920
and of course, this guy.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.100
Oscar Isaac plays Blue Jones, an orderly at
the mental asylum who becomes a slick pimp

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.680
in Babydoll's fantasy world.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:48.340
The dual role gave Isaac lots of room to show
off his acting chops—and his sexy singing

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:48.840
voice.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:50.440
Drive

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.160
There's no denying that Ryan Gosling is the
star of Drive, but Oscar Isaac is pivotal

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.760
to the plot of this 1970s-style thriller.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.520
Isaac plays an ex-con who's hoping to start
his life anew and set things right with his

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:07.780
wife, played by Carey Mulligan — which is
complicated by the presence of Gosling as

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.840
the cool, quiet neighbor on whom she's developed
a crush.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.520
Hey, she's only human.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.340
Despite a tense introduction, the two men
end up collaborating on a criminal scheme,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.740
but Isaac's character doesn't make to the
end credits.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.720
However, his death sets off a chain of events
that culminates in the most gruesome elevator

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.380
beatdown in the history of film.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.480
The Bourne Legacy

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.400
What the fourth Bourne film lacks in Matt
Damon, it makes up in Oscar Isaac.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.260
This time, the up-and-comer plays the enigmatic
Number Three, a government operative who's

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.800
being punished for falling in love on the
job.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.680
Relocated to a cabin in the middle of Alaska,
he survives just long enough on-screen to

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.240
give star Jeremy Renner a helping hand before
being wiped out by an assassin drone.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.540
Inside Llewyn Davis

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.660
Written and directed by the Coen brothers,
this is the movie that finally put Oscar Isaac

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.180
on the radar of Hollywood and audiences alike.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.800
He earned a Golden Globe nomination for his
performance as the title character, a musician

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.160
struggling to make it in New York's folk music
scene.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:12.180
Whether he's auditioning for a hard-bitten
music producer, verbally sparring with John

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.410
Goodman, or carrying a cat through Manhattan,
you can't help but root for this guy.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.980
"Llewyn is the cat."

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:20.540
"No, Llewyn HAS the cat."

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.219
Plus, you've gotta love watching Oscar Isaac
perform a duet with Adam Driver long before

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.400
they met again as enemies in The Force Awakens.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.740
A Most Violent Year

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:38.300
Set in 1981, this movie from 2014 finds Oscar
Isaac playing Abel Morales, an immigrant businessman

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.460
with big plans and even bigger problems.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.760
The owner of a heating oil company, Abel plans
on taking control of the Big Apple, but first,

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:50.120
he needs to raise enough money for a pricey
piece of real estate, tangle with a district

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.720
attorney, and discover who's stealing his
oil.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.919
With the beautiful and brutal Jessica Chastain
egging him on, Isaac's conflicted character

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.840
has to decide if he should take the high road
or another kind of path.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:03.720
Ex Machina

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.639
Oscar Isaac is at his bad guy best here playing
a tech genius named Nathan, a man with the

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.900
freaky ambitions of a Frankenstein and the
tech savvy of a Zuckerberg.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.100
In a movie where everyone is on their A game
— including Alicia Vikander in the role

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:23.060
of a seductive robot — Isaac completely
steals the show, oozing with menace and contempt,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.860
bullying his AI creation, and tearing up the
dance floor in one of the movie's most memorable

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:28.460
scenes.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.780
X-Men: Apocalypse

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.189
No Hollywood resume is complete without a
superhero flick, and Oscar Isaac gave the

00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:40.640
genre a go with this one — in which he plays
a blue-skinned mutant with a serious God complex.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:46.420
After napping for a couple of millennia, Apocalypse
emerges from his tomb, decides he hates the

00:05:46.439 --> 00:05:50.699
'80s, and prepares for world domination…
only to discover that he's stumbled into one

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.040
of the worst films of the X-Men franchise.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.360
Yeah, critics hated X-Men: Apocalypse, and
American audiences weren't crazy about it,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.000
either, meaning and poor Isaac spent hours
under all that makeup only to have his character

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.219
criticized as "an incredible letdown".

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:08.999
But while h ce didn't find success as a mutant,
Oscar Isaac is set for life as a Star Wars

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.680
hero, so it all works out in the end.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:11.940
Thanks for watching!

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.340
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:16.600
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

